{"kind":"request","text":"Reporting artifact: 6160734fa630a8dd9f0437c7cebfc7874825b74d (internal/app/association.go at 557ef676, newest on promise a4c569b1). Codex: fresh independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of stage S1, at exact head 557ef676d00cd225e72aa9649c0426ad65121159 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed). This head answers your second changes-requested verdict, 91c50bf9 (full id git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:91c50bf9d8ef0c48cb0f855fe0324a30434f14d8) on 643ab9c5, against reporting artifact 94e9d744, ratified 2d590023. It also carries the three corrections from your first verdict, 19b37887 on 0d1c7e11, ratified 6803776f. Those are two separate events; I give the full ids because a prefix of one spliced to a suffix of the other names neither.\n\nWhat governs it. Child request ac54fe71 (#22310), \"S1: deliver the governing design and read-only checkout association\", a stage child of #22168, itself governed by Hugh's effective adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b (#22208) and the reconciled design. My promise on that child is a4c569b1, unchanged: the outcome, conditions, performer, destination and governing authority are the same, so the corrections stayed on the existing commitment rather than becoming a new one. The sibling stages are 0e7c04c8 (#22311, S2) and 8415ce89 (#22312, S3); neither is delivered or claimed here.\n\nWhat to read first. The reporting artifact is the internal/app/association.go artifact, published last among the artifacts resting on the promise. It states what each verdict asked for and what changed, then the conditions one by one, the proof-obligation to test table, the twenty-two omission controls and the gates. The path set is now TWENTY, three more than last round: internal/app/checkout_read.go and internal/app/checkout_read_test.go are new files, and internal/app/landing_graph.go is an existing file this head modifies, so its artifact succeeds a live in-target one rather than opening a new path. Nineteen companion artifacts stand at this same head, one per changed path; the ten whose path changed since 643ab9c5 -- internal/app/checkout_read.go, internal/app/checkout_read_test.go, internal/app/landing_graph.go, internal/app/association.go, internal/app/association_test.go, internal/app/worktree_landing.go, internal/service/ui.go, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/landing-observations.md and notes/2026-09-09-lane-identity-reconciled.md -- each cite this verdict and say what changed at that path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE 643ab9c5: ten files, +523/-78, and what was missing was the governing condition rather than a rule.\n\nYou found that handleWorktrees called ClassifyWorktrees and Associations independently, each opening its own three-second deadline, allocating its own 65,536-step budget and running its own for-each-ref; that these are two observations with equal numeric bounds rather than one read; and that the previous report answered by calling the condition met \"over shared inputs\", which reconciles nothing. I agree, and I have not tried to resolve it by changing the description again.\n\nThe fix. internal/app/checkout_read.go is new: CheckoutRead holds the one captured observation -- checkout listing, bounded ref inventory and whether that read finished inside its bound, remote name, one three-second deadline, one 65,536-step budget. CaptureCheckouts takes it for a request; captureAround takes the same capture around a listing a caller already holds. internal/app/landing_graph.go splits readLandingRefs into readRefInventory, the one bounded for-each-ref, and newLandingGraph, a traversal over an already captured inventory; readLandingRefs remains their composition, so existing callers are unchanged. ClassifyCheckouts and AssociateCheckouts each read read.Worktrees, read.refs and read.remoteName and spend read.budget under read.ctx. handleWorktrees captures once and calls both. ClassifyWorktrees and Associations are kept for their existing callers, implemented on captureAround, so nobody gets a second read by using the convenient shape. The association charges the shared budget before it answers anything, including from cache, because a judgment that finds the read already spent has to say so.\n\nOne behaviour change I want you to look at squarely, because it is a change and not a refactoring: the budget now bounds the request rather than each judgment. A request that had 65,536 steps for classification and another 65,536 for the association now has 65,536 in total, and under exhaustion both judgments say unknown. Unknown protects a checkout and proves no negative, so the direction is conservative, but a very large repository that formerly answered both questions may now answer neither. Both documentation pages state it. If you read the assigned condition as wanting shared inputs with per-judgment allowances instead, say so and I will take it to adoption rather than choose.\n\nWhat did not change is the separation your security conclusion rests on: what is shared is the observation and the bound, never the conclusion. The classifier reads no grade and no association table; the association reads no classification. TestSharingTheReadDoesNotShareAuthority is the control, and your four cleanup controls still pass.\n\nTests, in internal/app/checkout_read_test.go: TestOneCapturedReadServesBothJudgments, which counts the git subprocesses that actually ran through a shim ahead of git on PATH -- exactly one for-each-ref and at most one worktree list per captured read -- because a call graph can be read wrongly and an in-process counter measures the seam a test author chose rather than the work the machine did; TestBothJudgmentsAnswerAboutTheCapturedRefs, which moves a branch after the capture and holds that neither judgment notices; TestTheSharedBudgetIsSpentByBothJudgments; TestSharingTheReadDoesNotShareAuthority; TestTheSingleJudgmentEntryPointsUseOneCaptureEach. Mutants: m21-second-ref-read and m22-separate-budget, both killed.\n\nDocs. Architecture layer 7 and landing-observations both now open on one captured read answering the whole request, and both carry \"what is shared is the observation and the bound, never the conclusion\"; the four reports are described as over that captured read and the cache as keyed under it. The reconciled note's cleanup section opens the same way. You named notes/2026-09-09-lane-identity-changes-for-adoption.md:285 and :292-293 as still describing one shared table and read: that file is unchanged and now matches the implementation, which is the direction reconciliation had to go.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE 0d1c7e11, all of it still present: six files, +476/-72, then two more files and +15/-8 of comments. Restated because this is a fresh review of a head whose whole delta from main you have not seen at once. Each of your findings, its fix, its regression and its omission control:\n\nFinding 1, cache validity. Fix: associationCacheKey gains a third input, associationCheckoutDigest over each captured checkout's label, branch, head and detached flag in the order given -- inputs only, never the Grade and Governing the pass writes back, which would key the cache on its own output. copyAssociationTable now also clones each claim's Candidates slice, not just the outer claims slice, so a cached table hands no caller a shared candidate list. Ref move and rename invalidation is retained. Regression: TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo, which builds two real unreferenced heads carrying the same trailer, captures one snapshot, and holds that the second detached checkout gets its own answer rather than the first one's cached row at unchanged refs and an unchanged frontier. Control: m18-cache-drops-checkouts, the digest replaced with a constant.\n\nFinding 2, exhaustion must report incomplete. Fix: associationTips returns an exhausted flag and detects the excess before dropping anything; Store.Lineage asks git for one commit past the per-tip limit, drops the extra and names the cut-off tip in LineageResult.Truncated; associate turns either into an incomplete table with a stated reason, empty rows and every checkout unknown. Regressions: TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated, which builds real local branches and holds that 256 answer and 257 report incomplete, counting the branches through git rather than through the bound under test; and TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated, the same one level down at 512 and 513 first-parent commits. You noted the per-tip case as source inspection rather than an executed counterexample; it is executed now. Control: m19-exhaustion-signal-dropped.\n\nFinding 3, missing objects and the annotation step. Fix: Lineage separates unheldTip (\"bad object\", \"bad revision\") from emptyHistory (an unborn repository, a complete answer of nothing) and names unheld tips in LineageResult.Unavailable; associate discards the batch on any unavailable tip; completion is decided in exactly one place, incompleteAssociation, with Associations calling unknownAssociationTable on an incomplete result and applyAssociationRows returning early rather than annotating from empty rows, so the unknown you identified can no longer be cleared to blank on the way out. Regression: TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory, and TestAssociationDiscardsTheWholeBatchOnBudgetExhaustion gained a cancelled-context leg at the public entry point. Control: m20-bad-object-is-success.\n\nFinding 4, your Simplification point about the report's own prose. The earlier report claimed cleanup reads \"the same table\" under \"the same cache\" while handleWorktrees calls ClassifyWorktrees and Associations separately. Corrected in the report and in both documentation pages: the association and the cleanup classification are two judgments over shared inputs -- one captured checkout listing, the same bounded ref inventory, the same 65,536-step budget and three-second deadline -- with separate caches keyed differently, neither reading the other's result, kept apart so an unsigned claim cannot reach a deletion decision.\n\nThen one comment correction, at 643ab9c5 over 1d7f3656: two files, +15/-8, every changed line inside a comment. The declaration-site comment on the Workspace association-cache fields had gone on describing a two-input key after the third input was added for finding 1, and the doc comment on TestAssociationCachesUnderTheFrontierAndRefDigest said the same thing. Both now name all three inputs and record that the grades this pass writes back are outputs that never key the cache. That test's name is deliberately unchanged: the checkout-digest half has its own named proof in TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo, and the m10 mutant evidence cites the existing name.\n\nYour three probes from your own review file were re-run unmodified against this tree in a throwaway file and all pass. The file is not committed; the three regressions above are named for the defects rather than for you.\n\nEvidence attached: gates-s1-v4.txt and mutants-s1.txt.\n\nGates, eight, all rc=0 and all fresh at this head; nothing is carried this round. Each rc is the command's own exit status under set -o pipefail: gofmt -l ./internal ./cmd; go vet ./...; go build ./...; go test -count=1 over internal/app, internal/gitstore, internal/service, internal/reviewguard and internal/statusview; go test -race -count=1 ./internal/app/; go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/..., green on its first run this time, so the live-log frontier rollback I disclosed last round has nothing to disclose here; go test -count=1 ./...; git diff --check.\n\nMutants, twenty-two, all run against this consolidated tree. The twenty earlier controls were re-run and are still killed; the m10 and m19 patch scripts were rewritten to target the consolidated code, and the guard each removes is unchanged. m21 and m22 are new, one per part of this finding. None survived.\n\nWhat I am asking you to check. Your architecture and security conclusions on the earlier heads already covered the grading, the forged-trailer and copied-ident controls, exact-commit corroboration through the shared owned edge, foreign and unresolved handling, object re-verification, the absence of association-driven admission, the three-input cache, the incomplete-propagation paths and the cleanup protections, and you confirmed the first verdict's three defects corrected. This round is the shared read and whatever it disturbed:\n\n1. That one captured read really is one: one for-each-ref and one checkout listing per request, on both the two-judgment path and the kept single-judgment entry points, and that TestOneCapturedReadServesBothJudgments measures that rather than asserting it.\n2. That the shared budget is genuinely shared and genuinely bounds the request, and that exhaustion in either judgment leaves the other saying unknown rather than answering from an uncounted allowance.\n3. That sharing the observation shared no authority: the classifier still reads no grade and no association table, the association still reads no classification, and no unsigned claim can reach a deletion or admission decision by this route.\n4. Whether the request-level budget is the right reading of the assigned condition, given that it can now leave a very large repository answering neither question where it previously answered both. I have stated it rather than buried it; if it needs adoption, say so.\n5. That splitting readLandingRefs into readRefInventory and newLandingGraph changed no landing or classification behaviour, that two graphs over one shared inventory stay independent, and that a malformed or over-bound ref read still yields an unknown rather than a partial inventory.\n6. That the three documentation pages and the two notes now agree with the code and with each other, including the changes-for-adoption lines you named.\n7. That the head still assigns and claims no S2 or S3 outcome, and that parent #22168 stays open.\n\nOne thing I am telling you rather than letting you find it: ui/src/lib/api.ts is still deliberately not extended and the wireparity gate does not cover WorktreeView, so no gate catches drift there. You did not make that a blocker last round and I have not changed it.\n\nMerge base. This head is one commit over main 359b211c. Current main is c0108be8, twenty-one commits further on, and #22093 landed in that span. git merge-tree --write-tree main 557ef676 is clean, producing tree 8d41601c298e9e0f2b376f9fc9dc6aa95b581470, so no rebase or recut is needed; the gap is ordinary staleness of the reasoning, not a changed head. The seventeen artifacts published at 643ab9c5 are live at eighteen of these twenty paths but sit outside the merge target on this branch, so they are not bases of this set and this merge does not retire them; I retire them separately naming these as successors. In-target predecessors were re-derived from a projection at depth 22585: internal/app/app.go now has two, main having moved under it, and internal/app/landing_graph.go has one, which is why that path succeeds rather than opens.\n\nFile the verdict with gs review, naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first, so the lane resolves through the reporting artifact. I will ratify the report.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6160734fa630a8dd9f0437c7cebfc7874825b74d","conditions":"Fresh independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of the exact head 557ef676d00cd225e72aa9649c0426ad65121159 on request/lane-worktree-identity, against child request ac54fe71 (#22310) under the same promise a4c569b1 and the adopted design ba18cd1a/7a71886b, reading the twenty artifacts published at that head with the internal/app/association.go artifact as the reporting artifact. This head answers the second changes-requested verdict 91c50bf9 on 643ab9c5, ratified 2d590023, in ten files, +523/-78, and carries the three corrections from the first verdict 19b37887 on 0d1c7e11, ratified 6803776f. Confirm the governing condition is now delivered rather than redescribed: one captured read -- checkout listing, bounded ref inventory, remote name, one three-second deadline, one 65,536-step budget -- answers the whole request, both judgments read and spend from it, one for-each-ref and one checkout listing run per request on the two-judgment path and on the kept single-judgment entry points alike, and the association charges the shared budget before answering even from cache. Confirm the shared budget bounds the request, so exhaustion in either judgment leaves the other reporting unknown rather than answering from an uncounted allowance, and say whether that request-level bound is the right reading of the assigned condition given that a very large repository may now answer neither question where it previously answered both; that behaviour change is stated in the report and both documentation pages, and if it needs its own adoption, say so. State the three conclusions explicitly. Architecture: name the affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md, say whether the head preserves or changes their contract, confirm the same-head pages and both notes now agree with the code and with each other including the changes-for-adoption lines the verdict named, confirm that splitting readLandingRefs into readRefInventory and newLandingGraph changed no landing or classification behaviour and that two graphs over one shared inventory stay independent, and confirm the head neither delivers nor claims any outcome of stages 0e7c04c8 (#22311) or 8415ce89 (#22312) and does not close or narrow parent #22168. Security: confirm sharing the observation shared no authority -- the classifier reads no grade and no association table, the association reads no classification, and no unsigned claim reaches a deletion or admission decision by this route -- and that the earlier protections stand: corroboration only from a standing effective artifact plus the shared owned edge or adopted decision, no Git author ident read, objects re-verified before any trailer read, every bounded stop reporting incomplete rather than a shorter answer, and a malformed or over-bound ref read yielding unknown rather than a partial inventory. Simplification: identify any opportunity to simplify without weakening these conditions. File the verdict with gs review naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first. Attached: gates-s1-v4.txt, eight gates all rc=0 and all fresh at this head with no carried evidence and the docset gate green on its first run; and mutants-s1.txt, twenty-two omission controls run against this consolidated tree, twenty re-run and killed with the m10 and m19 patch scripts retargeted, plus m21-second-ref-read and m22-separate-budget, none surviving.","head":"557ef676d00cd225e72aa9649c0426ad65121159","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6160734fa630a8dd9f0437c7cebfc7874825b74d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:91c50bf9d8ef0c48cb0f855fe0324a30434f14d8, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac54fe712abc7ca0614ca1067bad1a7ff8c490b0 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 146 more","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}